# Copy to plutonium.env and change what you need. Every value here is a default.

# Chromium checkout (holds .gclient and src/) and depot_tools. About 100 GB
# with a build; put them on a fast disk.
#CHROMIUM_DIR=$HOME/chromium
#DEPOT_TOOLS=$HOME/depot_tools
# Build dir, relative to src/.
#CHROMIUM_OUT=out/Plutonium
# Dev builds of each checkout's patches, kept so that checkouts (worktrees)
# keep running their own while the out dir moves on; the newest are kept.
#BUILDS_DIR=$DATA_DIR/builds
#BUILDS_KEEP=6
# Copies of each shell part `plutonium live apply` keeps (besides the current
# and previous ones). A T3 Code web app is about 140 MB.
#LIVE_KEEP=5
# Compile through ccache. Going back to a patch set built before then costs a
# fraction of the build, but Chromium's clang modules go off, so a build
# nothing was cached for takes about 1.6x as long. Turning it on (or off)
# rebuilds everything once.
#CCACHE=1
#CCACHE_DIR=$DATA_DIR/ccache
#CCACHE_SIZE=50G
# Compile jobs at once. The default is one per 2 GiB of RAM, at most one per CPU.
#BUILD_JOBS=8
# The container that builds Chromium, if not CONTAINER.
#BUILD_CONTAINER=plutonium

# T3 Code checkout (only for the T3 frontend).
#T3CODE_DIR=$ROOT/shells/hydra/src/checkout
# 3773 (T3 Code's own default), plus 100 per slot.
#T3_PORT=3773

# Browser profiles (profile-drm, profile-x11) and the container's home.
#DATA_DIR=$HOME/.local/share/plutonium
# T3 Code's data (threads, settings, pairings): the host's own, as the T3 Code
# app and CLI use it ($T3CODE_HOME, else ~/.t3).
#T3_HOME=$HOME/.t3
# Where the T3 Code server listens. 127.0.0.1 is enough for T3 Connect and
# for 'plutonium hydra pair --tailscale'.
#T3_HOST=127.0.0.1
# Pair phones and other devices over your tailnet from Settings, Connections:
# Tailscale Serve publishes the server at https://<this machine>.<tailnet>.ts.net
# while it runs (needs tailscaled, and `sudo tailscale set --operator=$USER`).
#T3_TAILSCALE=1
#T3_TAILSCALE_PORT=443

# Run Ash on the host, with no container (Nix only for now): the release
# build (out/Release), with its libraries from nixpkgs. `plutonium host build` makes it.
#HOST=1
#HOST_CHROME=$DATA_DIR/host-chrome
#HOST_TOOLS=$DATA_DIR/host-tools

# The browser's DevTools: a Unix socket in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/plutonium/ that only
# your own processes can connect to (socket), or Chrome's --remote-debugging-port
# on 127.0.0.1, 9224 (X11 9223) + 100 per slot, which any local process of any
# user can drive the browser through (port). For tools that need a port.
#DEVTOOLS=socket

# The build and run container.
#CONTAINER=plutonium
#IMAGE=localhost/plutonium-build:24.04
# If podman says "cannot re-exec process to join the existing user namespace"
# (a shell inside another user namespace, such as an agent's sandbox):
#PODMAN=dev/pm.sh

# The shells the login screen entry starts (plutonium session install), instead
# of your installed ones.
#SESSION_SHELLS="niri plain"

# Apps' screen capture and screenshots (OBS, Firefox, Discord) go through the
# desktop portal to the helper: a DRM session points the user's
# xdg-desktop-portal at it (XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=Plutonium) and puts back what
# was there when it ends. 0 leaves the portal alone (docs/features/CAPTURE.md).
#PORTAL_SESSION=1

# X11 apps in threads: xwayland-satellite, if it isn't on PATH.
#PLUTONIUM_XWAYLAND_SATELLITE=/path/to/xwayland-satellite

# This checkout's slot (normally from .plutonium-slot, which `plutonium
# worktree add` writes): its sessions' ports are 100 x slot higher, and their
# sockets, profiles and Xvfb display have their own names. A worktree's own
# settings go in plutonium.local.env, read after plutonium.env.
#PLUTONIUM_SLOT=0
